<TopicPage slug="subjects/history/advanced/germany-1918-1945">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    This is Cambridge’s Depth Study B in full, and its four key questions are the lesson’s four
    acts: <strong>was the Weimar Republic doomed from the start? Why was Hitler able to dominate
    Germany by 1934? How effectively did the Nazis control what they ruled? And what was it like
    to live in Nazi Germany?</strong> The through-line is uncomfortable: a modern, cultured
    democracy was dismantled in eighteen months by legal means, with the help of an economic
    catastrophe — and replaced by a dictatorship whose logical destination was genocide. Keep
    asking the syllabus’s own question: how much of this was fate, and how much was choice?
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="Germany, 1918–1945: republic, collapse, dictatorship — chips mark the three eras" widget="timeline" config={timelineCfg} />

  <h2>Was the Weimar Republic doomed from the start?</h2>
  <p>
    The Republic was born of defeat and blamed for it: sailors mutinied at Kiel, the Kaiser
    fled, civilian politicians signed the armistice — and then Versailles arrived, with its
    Diktat, Article 231 and reparations. The “stab-in-the-back” myth was ready-made. The
    constitution was admirable and unloved: proportional representation fragmented the
    Reichstag into squabbling parties (more than a dozen governments in fourteen years),
    while Article 48 let the president rule by emergency decree — a constitutional route to
    dictatorship lying dormant. Threats came from the left (the Spartacist rising, crushed by
    Freikorps in January 1919) and the right (the Kapp Putsch, 1920). Then came 1923:
    French occupation of the Ruhr, passive resistance financed by the printing press, and
    hyperinflation that annihilated savings. Gustav Stresemann stabilised everything — the
    Rentenmark (November 1923), the Dawes Plan (1924) and Young Plan (1929), Locarno (1925),
    League membership (1926) — and the “golden twenties” gave Germany modernist theatre,
    Bauhaus design and cinema. But recovery rested on short-term American loans. Was Weimar
    doomed from the start? The evidence says: not doomed — but never defended by those with
    the power to defend it.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "proportional representation destroyed Weimar, so any
    republic with PR was doomed." The voting system fragmented parliament, but the republic
    survived six years of it — including hyperinflation and two putsches — and functioned
    well when its elites wanted it to. The fatal ingredient was not the electoral maths but
    the decision, from 1930, of conservative politicians, army leaders and industrialists to
    govern <em>around</em> parliament with Article 48 decrees. Democracy in Weimar died less
    by constitution than by abandonment: the men who ran the state stopped believing in it
    before the voters did.
  </p>

  <Widget no="2" caption="registered unemployment in Germany, annual averages, 1928–1938 (millions)" widget="plotter" config={unempCfg} />

  <h2>Why was Hitler able to dominate Germany by 1934?</h2>
  <p>
    Before 1929 the Nazis were a fringe: a 25-point programme (1920) mixing rage at Versailles
    with anti-Semitism and promises of a national community, a Führer principle, and SA
    street brawlers. The Munich Putsch of 8–9 November 1923 collapsed in a shoot-out; at his
    trial Hitler became famous, wrote <em>Mein Kampf</em> in prison, and drew the right
    conclusion — power through legality. Then the curve above happened: registered
    unemployment quadrupled between 1928 and 1932, peaking above six million in
    early 1932 — over a third of the workforce. Nazi votes tracked the misery: 2.6 per cent
    (1928), 18.3 (1930), 37.3 (July 1932). Crucially, by January 1933 the Nazi tide was
    actually ebbing (33.1 per cent in November 1932) — which is why the decision of
    conservative elites matters so much. Papen and Hindenburg’s circle believed they could
    <em>use</em> Hitler; Hitler was appointed Chancellor on 30 January 1933 and used them.
    Consolidation took eighteen months: the Reichstag Fire decree (February 1933) suspended
    rights and smashed the Communists; the Enabling Act (23 March) made his laws unreviewable;
    trade unions and parties were abolished by summer; the Night of the Long Knives (30 June
    1934) bought the army’s loyalty with the SA’s blood; and when Hindenburg died (2 August
    1934) soldiers swore a personal oath to Führer Hitler.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "Hitler seized power in a coup d'état." Almost every step
    was formally legal: appointment by the president under the constitution, an emergency
    decree under Article 48, an Enabling Act passed by the Reichstag with the required
    two-thirds majority, a plebiscite (November 1933) recording over 90 per cent approval in a
    climate of intimidation. Historians call it a "legal revolution" — legality was the
    disguise, and it mattered, because each step normalised the next and gave judges,
    civil servants and foreign chancelleries a reason to comply. The lesson generalises: a
    democracy is not usually overthrown against its rules but <em>through</em> them, by an
    opposition willing to use legality as a crowbar.
  </p>

  <h2>How effectively did the Nazis control Germany, 1933–45?</h2>
  <p>
    Control ran on both fear and enthusiasm. The police state: the SS under Himmler and the
    Gestapo’s network of informers, with concentration camps (Dachau from March 1933) for
    opponents — opposition was possible but lethal. Propaganda: Goebbels’s ministry (from
    March 1933) ran radio sets into every front room, Nuremberg rallies, film and
    book-burnings, selling the Volksgemeinschaft. The economy: rearmament, public works and
    the autobahn programme cut registered unemployment to near zero by 1938 — conscription
    and the removal of women from the statistics hid the rest; the Four-Year Plan (1936)
    made Germany ready for a war it could not afford peacefully. Youth were enrolled
    (Hitler Youth and the girls’ league, compulsory from 1936), the churches were conciliated
    then squeezed (the Concordat of 1933; minority resistance from the Confessing Church).
    And the persecution: boycotts (April 1933), the Nuremberg Laws (1935), Kristallnacht
    (1938), then war-time ghettos, the Einsatzgruppen’s shootings and the Wannsee Conference
    (January 1942) coordinating the Final Solution — about six million Jews murdered. Was
    Nazi Germany a totalitarian state? The apparatus reached everywhere; the honest
    qualification is that consent and fear were mixed in doses that historians still argue
    over — which is exactly why the syllabus leaves the question open.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "the Gestapo watched everyone, so the regime's control was
    total surveillance." The terror was efficient precisely because it was thin: historian
    Robert Gellately's studies of Bavaria found the Gestapo's caseload ran overwhelmingly on
    denunciations from ordinary neighbours, employers and even family members. Control was
    <em>co-produced</em>: a minority of fanatics, a larger minority of beneficiaries and
    enthusiasts, a majority that grumbled but complied, and a small, hunted minority of
    resisters. A regime with perhaps one Gestapo officer per several thousand citizens did
    not need total surveillance — only enough citizens willing to do its watching for it.
  </p>

  <h2>What was it like to live in Nazi Germany?</h2>
  <p>
    The experience depended entirely on who you were. For young people, the state wanted the
    whole childhood: the Hitler Youth and its girls' league (BDM), compulsory from 1936,
    supplied camping, sport, ritual and indoctrination, so that by the late 1930s most
    adolescents spent more waking hours in Nazi organisations than in school. Not everyone
    bought it: the Edelweiss Pirates of Cologne and the Ruhr camped, sang forbidden songs and
    mocked the league uniforms, and in Munich a student circle — the White Rose — distributed
    leaflets against the war and the deportations until Hans and Sophie Scholl were executed
    in February 1943. For women, policy ran backwards from the Weimar years: marriage loans
    (1933) paid couples to wed on condition the bride left work, the rhetoric celebrated
    Kinder, Küche, Kirche, and the Mother's Cross (1938) decorated maternity by output —
    bronze for four children, silver for six, gold for eight. For workers, the free trade
    unions were destroyed in May 1933 and replaced by the German Labour Front, which offered
    Strength Through Joy — cruises, theatre trips, the promise of a "people's car" that
    hundreds of thousands paid into through five-mark weekly savings and not one customer
    ever received — while real wages stayed below their 1929 level and the working week
    lengthened. Prosperity was real, unevenly distributed, and partly an accounting of
    women, conscripts and political prisoners removed from the jobless totals.
  </p>
  <p>
    The churches show the regime's texture best. Hitler signed a Concordat with the Catholic
    Church (July 1933) and courted the Protestants — then squeezed both: the Confessing
    Church of pastors like Dietrich Bonhoeffer and Martin Niemöller resisted the state takeover
    of the Protestant faith at real personal cost, and in 1937 the papal encyclical <em>Mit
    brennender Sorge</em>, smuggled into Germany and read from pulpits, condemned the regime's
    racial ideology. And the sharpest demonstration of control's limits came not from the
    churches but from their example: the regime's "euthanasia" programme — the murder of the
    disabled, begun secretly in 1939 — was publicly denounced by the Catholic bishop von
    Galen in August 1941 and officially halted, killing thereafter continued by less
    conspicuous means. Even a totalitarian state counted votes of public opinion where it
    feared them. For Jewish Germans, of course, the question of "life in Nazi Germany" was
    already a question of escape or survival: boycotts (April 1933), the Nuremberg Laws
    (1935), the confiscations and terror of Kristallnacht (1938) — by which point more than
    half of Germany's roughly half-million Jews had emigrated, and the window for those
    remaining was closing fast.
  </p>

  <Widget no="3" caption="check yourself: the syllabus's four key questions" widget="quiz" config={gerQuizCfg} />

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Intentionalism versus functionalism.</strong> Was the Holocaust the execution of a
    long-held master plan, or the product of chaotic radicalisation? "Intentionalists"
    (Bullock, Dawidowicz) point to Hitler's consistent antisemitism since 1919 and read
    1933–41 as unfolding intention; "functionalists" (Mommsen, Broszat) emphasise the polycratic
    regime's competing agencies, the improvised escalations — ghettos, then mass shootings,
    then death camps — and locate the decisive shift in 1941, when war in the east closed the
    emigration "solutions" and opened extermination. The current synthesis leans functionalist
    in mechanism, intentionalist in direction: no detailed gas-chamber blueprint from 1924,
    but a destination visible in every waypoint — which is why the Wannsee Conference of
    January 1942 reads less like a beginning than a coordination meeting.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Ordinary men, willing executioners.</strong> Christopher Browning's <em>Ordinary
    Men</em> (1992) reconstructed a reserve police battalion of middle-aged Hamburg workers
    who shot tens of thousands in Poland in 1942, and found no monsters among them — just
    conformity, careerism, alcohol and deference to authority; Daniel Goldhagen's
    <em>Hitler's Willing Executioners</em> (1996) counter-attacked that "eliminationist"
    antisemitism was a German national culture all along. The Goldhagen controversy, played
    out in packed lecture halls in 1996, remains the field's great argument about how far
    explanation may drift toward exculpation — and it forced the historiography to confront
    that the perpetrators were, overwhelmingly, unremarkable.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>The German Sonderweg and the Historikerstreit.</strong> The postwar "special path"
    thesis (Wehler and the Bielefeld school) read Nazism as the destination of Germany's
    incomplete modernisation — an aristocratic elite that kept authoritarian habits while the
    economy industrialised; later historians found comparable illiberalisms elsewhere and
    returned agency and contingency to 1933. In 1986 the <em>Historikerstreit</em> erupted when
    Ernst Nolte suggested the Holocaust was a reaction to Bolshevism — a relativisation Jürgen
    Habermas attacked as an attempt to normalise the past. The argument is not academic
    housekeeping: every country's memory politics — monuments, textbooks, guilt, comparison —
    lives downstream of it, and Germany's own unusually rigorous culture of public reckoning
    is its outcome. Economic historians add the last layer: Adam Tooze's <em>The Wages of
    Destruction</em> (2006) showed the Nazi economy was always rationing against bankruptcy,
    making the gambles of 1939–41 decisions taken from resource weakness as much as ideological
    hunger — a reminder that even the century's most ideological regime lived inside
    spreadsheet constraints.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Cambridge IGCSE History 0470 syllabus (2027–2028), Depth Study B: Germany, 1918–45; Evans, The Coming of the Third Reich; Kolb, The Weimar Republic.</p>
